==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|smoc|smoc(c)}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*smukkaz||something slipped into}}; akin to {{cog|goh|smocho}}, {{cog|is|smokkr}}, and from the root of {{cog|ang|smugan||to creep}}, akin to {{cog|de|schmiegen||to cling to, press close}}. {{cog|gmh|smiegen}}, {{cog|is|smjúga||to creep through, to put on a garment which has a hole to put the head through}}; compare with {{cog|lt|smukti||to glide}}. See also {{m|en|smug}}, {{m|en|smuggle}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/smɑk/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒk|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A woman's [[undergarment]]; a [[shift]]; a [[chemise]].
#* '''14th century''', [[w:Chaucer|Chaucer]], [[s:The Canterbury Tales|The Canterbury Tales]], [[s:The Canterbury Tales/The Clerk's Prologue and Tale|The Clerk's Prologue and Tale]]
#*: Before the folk herselfe stripped she,
#*: And in her '''smock''', with foot and head all bare,
#*: Toward her father's house forth is she fare.
# A [[blouse]]; a [[smock frock]].
#* '''1837''' {{w|Thomas Carlyle}}, ''{{w|The French Revolution: A History}}''
#*: And women were in that gabarre [boat]; whom the Red Nightcaps were stripping naked; who begged, in their agony, that their '''smocks''' might not be stript from them.
# A loose [[garment]] worn as [[protection]] by a [[painter]], etc.

